The '''imperial history of Burawa''' encompasses the period from the defeat of the [[Lake Confederation]] circa 1180 to the founding of [[Lacashe]], the first Burawi overseas colony in [[Abyala]], in 1516. Over approximately three and a half centuries, the [[Kingdom of Burawa]] consolidated control over the [[Southwestern Kaftia|Southwestern Kaftian]] peninsula, developed from a land-based feudal state into a maritime power, and established its first territorial claims on a continent previously unknown to [[Kaftia|Kaftian]] civilisation.

The period was governed throughout by [[House Kadim]], the dynasty restored to the Burawi throne circa 960. However, the Imperial era saw the emergence of three named '''cadet branches''' under the '''[[Laanta Boqorka]]''' ([[Burawi language|Burawi]]: "Branches of the King") system — [[Kadim-Kaysoor]], [[Kadim-Mazar]], and [[Kadim-Galwa]] — which competed for the throne through marriage alliances, [[Burawi Catholic Church|Church]] endorsement, and [[Hoggaanka|Hoga]] coalition-building. Nine kings ruled during the period, and the capital was relocated from [[Kaysoor]] to [[Samara]] in the late 15th century.

== Consolidation (1180–1268) ==

=== Defeat of the Lake Confederation and the Kingdom of Shedou ===

The Imperial era began with the conclusion of the campaigns that had characterised the final decades of the [[Medieval History of Burawa|medieval period]]. '''[[Andrias II]]''' (r. 1161–1195), who had overseen the defeat of the [[Lake Confederation]] circa 1180, continued to reign into the early Imperial period. The surrender of '''[[Lamido]] Yero''', the last paramount chief of the Confederation, brought the northern basin of [[Southwestern Kaftia]] under Burawi control.

The [[Kingdom of Shedou]], a [[Sarafoni people|Sarafoni]] remnant polity of the former Lake Confederation in the far northwest of the peninsula near [[Sarfone]] and [[Wolonia]], was conquered by military campaign circa 1200 under Andrias II's successor '''[[Guled II]]''' (r. 1195–1232). '''[[Buur Lat Sukaabe]]''', the last ruler of Shedou, was defeated and the kingdom dissolved. With the fall of Shedou, the [[Kingdom of Orma]] remained the sole independent polity on the peninsula.

=== Christianisation and Burawisation of the north ===

The conquest of the northern basin was followed by a programme of rapid cultural and religious assimilation. The [[Burawi Catholic Church]] established missions, parishes, and monasteries across the former [[Lake Confederation]] territories, and [[Ndogalu]], the indigenous religious tradition of the northern peoples, was actively suppressed. Resistance was localised and sporadic; no major revolt against the Christianisation programme is recorded.

The broader '''[[Burawisation]]''' policy, first applied during the [[Dagaalka Sago]] of the medieval period, was extended to the north on a larger scale. The [[Burawi language]] was imposed as the language of administration and law, Burawi legal institutions replaced local custom, and settler families from the southern coast and the Buran River valley were relocated to the newly incorporated territories. The programme was administered through a combination of royal governors in the central regions and newly appointed [[Hoggaanka|Hoga]] lords on the frontier.

=== The First Orma-Burawa War (1204) ===
{{main|First Orma-Burawa War}}

In 1204, the [[Kingdom of Orma]], the sole remaining independent polity on the peninsula, besieged and captured the city of [[Badalga]]. The conflict arose from a series of border disputes over the territories of [[Maianka]] and [[Sabia]], compounded by Orma's assessment that Burawi administrative resources were concentrated on the northern integration and the eastern frontier was consequently vulnerable.

Orma held Badalga for approximately two years before Burawi forces recaptured the city. The war concluded with the restoration of the status quo ante bellum. In response to the loss of Badalga, [[Guled II]] initiated a programme of defensive fortification along the eastern frontier, upgrading highland garrisons and establishing permanent border detachments along the Orma-Burawa boundary.

=== Establishment of the cadet houses ===

'''[[Nuur II]]''' (r. 1232–1268) formalised the '''[[Laanta Boqorka]]''' ([[Burawi language|Burawi]]: "Branches of the King"), the {{w|Cadet branch|cadet house}} system that characterised Imperial Burawi dynastic politics throughout the period. Through a series of marriages between the Kadim line and the principal regional power centres, Nuur established three named branches of the dynasty:

* '''[[Kadim-Kaysoor]]''': The main royal branch, seated at the capital of [[Kaysoor]]. Retained direct control of the central state — the [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]] coast, the [[Buran River]] valley, and the former [[Kingdom of Sago|Sago]] lowlands.
* '''[[Kadim-Galwa]]''': Founded by the royal appointment of a Kadim prince as governor of [[Galwa]] and the northern frontier. The branch administered the lakeside [[Hoggaanka|Hoga]] territories and the former [[Lake Confederation]] lands.
* '''[[Kadim-Mazar]]''': Formed through the intermarriage of the Kadim line with the old [[Mazar Clique]] merchant families. Based in [[Mazar]], the branch controlled the kingdom's [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]] commercial networks and, in later centuries, its naval policy.

The Laanta Boqorka served a dual purpose: it bound the kingdom's principal regions to the dynasty through ties of kinship, and it provided a regulated mechanism for the succession when the direct line of any branch failed to produce a suitable heir. Under the system, when a reigning branch could not furnish an heir — whether through death, infirmity, or political disqualification — the [[Burawi Catholic Church]] was empowered to broker the accession of a candidate from one of the other two branches. The Church's role as constitutional arbiter in these transitions extended its political influence throughout the Imperial era.

== Hoggaanka peak and the maritime turn (1268–1355) ==

=== Kadim-Mazar ascendancy ===

The death of [[Nuur II]] in 1268 produced the first invocation of the [[Laanta Boqorka]] succession mechanism. Nuur's only surviving son had died in a hunting accident several years earlier, and his grandsons were considered too young to govern. The [[Burawi Catholic Church]] brokered the transition to [[Kadim-Mazar]], and '''[[Petros II]]''' (r. 1268–1311) acceded to the throne with the endorsement of the Church and the support of the [[Mazar]] merchant establishment. Petros II was the first king to have been raised primarily within the commercial networks of Mazar rather than the royal court of Kaysoor, and his accession reflected a reorientation of the kingdom toward maritime trade and [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]] diplomacy.

=== The Reige personal union ===
{{main|Kingdom of Reige}}

The principal diplomatic achievement of the Kadim-Mazar period was the '''Reige marriage'''. '''[[Bernat of Reige]]''', prince of the [[Kingdom of Reige]], a Mesogean island kingdom situated between [[Southwestern Kaftia]] and the [[Volisanians|Volisanian]] coast, married '''[[Amina Kadim]]''', a princess of the [[Kadim-Kaysoor|Kadim]] line, creating a '''personal union''' between Burawa and Reige. The island, whose population was ethnically [[Volisanians|Volisanian]], retained its own lords and institutions under the arrangement, but Burawa gained a strategic foothold in the central [[Mesogean Sea]].

The personal union expanded Burawa's access to Mesogean trade networks and provided a forward base for diplomatic contact with the Illypnian kingdoms. The arrangement did not constitute a new cadet house; Reige remained institutionally separate from the Kadim dynastic structure.

The union had lasting consequences. In the early 17th century, the lords of Reige transferred their allegiance to the [[United Kingdoms of Volisania]], precipitating the '''[[War of the Mesogean]]''' between Burawa and Volisania.

=== The Second Orma-Burawa War ===
{{main|Second Orma-Burawa War}}

Under '''[[Simeon II]]''' (r. 1311–1355), renewed border conflict with the [[Kingdom of Orma]] developed over the contested territories of [[Maianka]] and [[Sabia]]. The '''[[Second Orma-Burawa War]]''' concluded without a decisive result: neither side achieved a significant territorial advantage, and the war ended with minor border adjustments and a negotiated truce. The outcome confirmed the pattern established in 1204, in which Orma retained sufficient military capacity to resist full incorporation into the Burawi state.

=== Development of the navy ===

The principal development of Simeon II's reign was the establishment of [[Badalga]] as a major shipbuilding centre. The deep bay at the tip of the Wagada Peninsula, combined with the city's access to both [[Lake Haro]] and the [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]] coast, made it an ideal site for the construction of ocean-going vessels. Investment from [[Iskhal]] merchants funded the establishment of shipbuilding firms, and by the mid-14th century Badalga had become the primary naval shipyard of the kingdom.

The expansion of the navy represented a structural change in Burawi military capability. The medieval kingdom had relied on the [[Republic of Mazar|Mazar]] merchant fleet for its limited naval capacity; the Imperial state developed a purpose-built blue-water navy capable of sustained operations in the open [[Aethiopean Ocean]]. This capacity was a prerequisite for the circumnavigation attempts and colonial expeditions of the following centuries.

== The outward turn (1355–1491) ==

=== Kadim-Kaysoor restored ===

[[Simeon II]] died in 1355 without a male heir. His daughters were excluded from the succession under the [[Laanta Boqorka]] conventions then in force, and the [[Kadim-Mazar]] branch could not furnish an alternative candidate of sufficient seniority. The [[Burawi Catholic Church]] brokered a restoration of [[Kadim-Kaysoor]], and '''[[Marcel III]]''' (r. 1355–1398) acceded to the throne. Marcel III's reign was characterised by an outward orientation: continued investment in the navy, the first attempts to circumnavigate [[Kaftia]], and the expansion of trade routes to the east.

Marcel III also formalised the [[Hoggaanka|Hoggaanka]] status of [[Badalga]], granting the city to the '''[[Hedga Clique]]''' under its first lord, '''[[Iohn Hedga]]'''. The grant recognised Badalga's strategic and economic importance and integrated the city's shipbuilding industry into the Hoggaanka military-obligations framework.

=== Circumnavigation attempts ===
{{main|Exploration of Northern Kaftia}}

From the mid-14th century onward, Burawi expeditions attempted to circumnavigate the continent of [[Kaftia]] in order to reach the [[Kendriyan Ocean]] and, through it, the markets of [[Fosia]]. The objective was to establish direct maritime trade routes to the kingdoms of eastern [[Fosia]] — primarily the [[Kingdom of Zong]] — without reliance on overland intermediaries. The urgency of these ventures was heightened by the contraction of the Fosian luxury trade that had reached [[Illypnia]] and the [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]] world through overland routes across [[Fosia]]. The '''[[Jade Sea War]]''' (c. 1300–1390), fought between the [[Kingdom of Daras|Daras]] and the [[Kingdom of Zong]], had disrupted the eastern terminus of these trade networks; the subsequent Darasian blockade reduced the supply of silks, ceramics, and spices reaching western markets and drove up their prices.

The geographic structure of Kaftia made the circumnavigation extraordinarily difficult. The continent's shape — a double-sided hammer, with the [[Southwestern Kaftia|Southwestern]], Northwestern, and Northeastern peninsulas extending from a narrow central body — required a voyage of approximately 23,000 kilometres from northern Burawa to the Kendriyan Ocean. Expeditions launched from the [[Aethiopean Ocean]] coast had to round the Southwestern peninsula, navigate the sparsely charted Northwestern coast, and then pass the Northeastern peninsula before entering the Kendriyan.

Three expeditions are of particular note. '''[[Mahad Qorrax]]''', sailing under Marcel III in the 1370s, rounded the Southwestern peninsula but turned back upon reaching the Northwestern coast, citing supply shortages and unfavourable currents. '''[[Liibaan Geel]]''', commissioned by Teodor III in the 1410s, reached the Northeastern peninsula — further than any prior expedition — but lost approximately half his fleet to storms and was compelled to return. '''[[Cabdi Samara]]''', dispatched in the 1460s, completed approximately two-thirds of the route before being recalled by the crown.

No expedition completed the full circumnavigation during the Imperial period. The accumulated cartographic knowledge from these voyages nevertheless contributed to Burawi understanding of the Kaftian coastline, and a complete circumnavigation was not achieved until the 17th century.

=== Trade routes to Fosia ===

In the absence of a viable circumnavigation route, Burawi merchants developed alternative paths to the Fosian markets:

* '''The northern sea route''': Ships sailed from the [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]] coast to the [[Triphylian Isthmus]], the narrow land bridge connecting [[Kaftia]] and [[Illypnia]] that separates the Mesogean Sea from the [[Kendriyan Ocean]]. Goods were transported overland across the isthmus, loaded onto ships on the Kendriyan coast, and sailed to the ports of [[Paran]] and [[Kemal]]. From there, overland caravan routes — the '''northern silk road''' — connected to the interior kingdoms of [[Fosia]].
* '''The southern route''': Trade goods were shipped from [[Mazar]] or [[Reige]] to the ports of southern [[Volisanians|Volisania]], then transported overland through [[Merica]] and [[Breisland]] to the south, and onward through modern day [[Kalinova]] and [[Daras]].
* '''The trans-Kaftian route''': Overland caravans passed north through [[Ayorin]] and across the [[Tamazgha]] desert, a distance of approximately 1,500 kilometres at the narrowest point, facilitated by the [[Imfezzans|Imfezzan]] peoples who inhabited the desert region. This route was the most difficult but provided access to northern Kaftian markets and, indirectly, to the Illypnian trade network.

=== The Third Orma-Burawa War ===
{{main|Third Orma-Burawa War}}

The '''[[Third Orma-Burawa War]]''', fought during the reign of [[Teodor III]], was the largest of the three Orma-Burawa conflicts. Burawa attempted for the first time to vassalise the [[Kingdom of Orma]] outright, committing the full resources of the royal army and Hoga levies to the campaign.

The campaign did not achieve its objective. Orma's military capacity was reinforced by diplomatic support from [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]] states, particularly [[Volisanians|Volisania]] and [[Echia]], both of which regarded an independent Orma as a counterbalance to Burawi hegemony in southern Kaftia. Marriage alliances between the Orma royal house and Mesogean noble families further strengthened Orma's position. The war concluded with Orma's independence intact and the balance-of-power dynamic between Burawa and the Mesogean states reaffirmed.

=== Decline of the Hoggaanka ===

The outward turn altered the internal balance of power within the kingdom. As crown revenue came increasingly to derive from maritime trade, port taxes, and the commercial activity of [[Mazar]] and [[Samara]], the economic importance of the northern frontier [[Hoggaanka|Hoga]] territories diminished. The Hoga lords retained their lands, military obligations, and local authority, but the crown's dependence on agricultural tribute and Hoga levies was reduced.

The crown's approach during this period was to integrate the Hoga families into the dynasty through marriage. Kadim princes and princesses were married into the principal Hoga houses, creating ties of kinship that linked feudal authority to dynastic loyalty. The Hoggaanka aristocracy was consequently brought into closer alignment with the crown, though frontier Hogas — particularly those associated with [[Kadim-Galwa]] — retained considerable autonomy.

== The Age of Discovery (1491–1516) ==

=== Kadim-Galwa and the new capital ===

The [[Third Orma-Burawa War]] weakened [[Kadim-Kaysoor]]'s political position: the failure to vassalise [[Kingdom of Orma|Orma]] was attributed to the branch's leadership, and the direct Kaysoor line lacked a suitable heir following the death of [[Teodor III]]. The [[Burawi Catholic Church]] brokered a transition, and '''[[Axmed II]]''' (r. 1452–1491) of [[Kadim-Galwa]] acceded to the throne with the support of the northern [[Hoggaanka|Hoga]] families. It was the first time the Galwa branch held the kingship. Axmed II's experience in the northern frontier administration informed a reign oriented toward outward expansion. His principal domestic act was the '''relocation of the capital from [[Kaysoor]] to [[Samara]]'''. [[Samara]], situated in the [[Galbeed River]] valley, possessed a natural harbour, occupied a more central position within the expanded kingdom, and provided direct access to the [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]] trade routes. [[Kaysoor]] retained its status as a religious and cultural centre, but the administrative seat of the kingdom was transferred to Samara.

=== The discovery of Lurandia ===
{{main|Discovery of Lurandia}}

[[Volisanians|Volisanian]] navigators, seeking westward trade routes to [[Fosia]], made landfall on the continent of '''[[Lurandia]]''', a large landmass situated due west of [[Illypnia]] across the [[Aethiopean Ocean]]. [[Volisanians|Volisania]] and [[Zakan]] were the first to establish contact with the indigenous peoples of Lurandia, initially through trading posts rather than formal colonies. [[Echia]], [[Merica]], [[Breisland]], and [[Aetolia]] established their own territorial claims in subsequent decades.

The broader [[Burawi Catholic Church|Catholic]] institutional structure declared the indigenous peoples of the newly discovered continents to be legitimate subjects for conversion, a pronouncement that sanctioned colonial expansion as a religious obligation. The declaration accelerated the pace at which Illypnian powers moved to establish territorial claims in both Lurandia and [[Abyala]].

=== The westward pivot ===
{{main|Discovery of Abyala}}

The Volisanian discovery presented Burawa with a strategic reassessment. The kingdom had committed considerable resources to the circumnavigation of [[Kaftia]], a route that had proved impractical. The distance from the northern coast of [[Southwestern Kaftia]] (the former [[Kingdom of Shedou|Shedou]] territory) to the eastern coast of [[Abyala]], a second previously unknown continent situated to the northwest, was approximately 3,900 kilometres, compared with the 23,000-kilometre circumnavigation route.

Under '''[[Dalgayan III]]''' (r. 1491–1520+), the crown redirected its maritime resources westward. The naval capacity and cartographic knowledge accumulated from the circumnavigation expeditions were applied to the shorter transatlantic crossing.

=== Discovery of the Badweyn Islands (1512) ===
{{main|Badweyn Islands}}

In 1512, an expedition led by the navigator '''[[Guled Badweyn]]''', sailing from the northern coast of the former [[Kingdom of Shedou|Shedou]] territory, crossed approximately 3,300 kilometres of the [[Aethiopean Ocean]] and made landfall on an uninhabited archipelago situated approximately 600 kilometres south of the [[Abyala|Abyalan]] mainland. Badweyn explored the islands over a period of several months before returning to [[Southwestern Kaftia]] to report his findings to [[Dalgayan III]].

The '''[[Badweyn Islands]]''', named after their discoverer, constituted the first Kaftian contact with [[Abyala]]. The crown recognised the strategic value of the discovery and granted Badweyn a royal charter to return with a larger fleet.

=== The founding of Lacashe (1516) ===
{{main|Burawi Cusub}}

In 1515, Badweyn's second expedition reached the [[Badweyn Islands]] and established a small outpost. The following year, the fleet pushed approximately 600 kilometres north to the [[Abyala|Abyalan]] mainland, making landfall on the eastern coast near the southeastern "hump" of the continent. The expedition established '''[[Lacashe]]''' ([[Burawi language|Burawi]]: "the wealthy coast"), the first permanent Burawi settlement on the mainland, on a coastal strip east of the [[Matarrañya-Cañadapirén Range]].

The settlers encountered indigenous peoples at the fringes of two distinct polities: a complex of plains kingdoms with tributary chiefdoms and extensive trade networks in the lowlands west of the mountains, and a highland empire further north along the mountain range. The coastal zone where Lacashe was founded lay at the intersection of trade between these two systems.

Lacashe formed the nucleus of '''[[Burawi Cusub]]''' ([[Burawi language|Burawi]]: "New Burawa"), the collective term for the Burawi colonial territories on the eastern coast of Abyala. Over the following decades, Burawi control expanded northward along the coast into the territories that correspond to modern [[Asikyira]] and parts of [[Balisca]].

The founding of Lacashe marked the end of the Imperial period and the beginning of the [[Colonial History of Burawa|Colonial era]]. The colony subsequently developed into a major source of wealth for the kingdom through the extraction of gold and agricultural production, and became a principal destination for the [[Kaftian slave trade]] that expanded in the decades following its establishment.

== Dynastic politics ==

The kingdom was governed throughout the Imperial period by [[House Kadim]], the dynasty restored to the throne circa 960. The Imperial era saw the emergence of three named cadet branches, each associated with a principal power base within the kingdom.

